# Alternating groups `𝔄ₙ`: gate type, circuit generators, and the relations of Lafont's Theorem 4

Translation of `Examples/Groups/Alternating/Syntactics.agda`.

Y. Lafont, Towards an algebraic theory of Boolean circuits, J. Pure Appl.
Algebra 184 (2003) 257–310, Section 2.4.  The even permutations form a
monoidal subcategory `𝔄` of the permutations, generated by the cyclic
permutation `ω : 3 → 3` of three adjacent wires, and Theorem 4 says that `ω`
and three relations present it.  Here that is a circuit presentation: `ω` is
a three-wire gate, the interchange law of the monoidal category is `Lift`'s
structural rules (`Lift.comm3` is Lafont's `ω_i ω_j = ω_j ω_i` for
`j > i + 2`), and at width `n` the circuits are the words in his generators
`ω₁, …, ω_{n−2}` of `𝔄ₙ`.

Conventions.  Wire 0 is the paper's leftmost wire, a generator `g.up` is the
paper's `id₁ | g`, and words are in diagram order: `w * v` is `w` then `v`,
which the paper writes `v ∘ w` and draws with `w` on top.  So `ω` sends the
wire at position 0 to 1, 1 to 2 and 2 to 0; drawn on p. 267, it is the
crossing of the wires `1 2` followed by that of `0 1`.

A `Theory` is a family of relations at every width (the framework's `CRel`),
and the Agda's `T ∣ n ⊢ w ≈ v`, the congruence that `Lift T` generates at
width `n`, is the framework's `Derives T n w v`.

**Deviations from the Agda.**  The Agda's `Gen n`, `Circuit n` are `Gn n`,
`Circ n` (the framework's `Gen`, `Circuit` at this gate set), `g ↥` is
`Gen.up g` and `w ↑` is `Circuit.shift w`; `ω-gen` is
`Gen.gate3 Gate.ω_gate`.  The width patterns (`₅₊`) are not needed: widths
are written `n + 5`.  The Agda's re-exports from `Lift-Relation` (`srel`,
`cong↑`, `comm₃`, `lemma-cong↑`, `comm-gate₃-w↑↑↑`, …) are the framework's
`Lift.rule`, `Lift.shift`, `Lift.comm3` and `CircuitProp.lemma_cong_up`,
`CircuitProp.comm_gate₃_w`.
-/
import QCert.Alt16RevCir.Lib.CircuitProp

namespace QCert.Alt16RevCir.Alternating

open QCert QCert.Circuit

/-! ## Gate type -/

/-- The one generating gate of `𝔄ₙ`: the cyclic permutation of three
adjacent wires. -/
inductive Gate : ℕ → Type
  | ω_gate : Gate 3

/-! ## Syntactic framework -/

/-- The generators of `n`-wire circuits (the Agda's `Gen n`). -/
abbrev Gn (n : ℕ) : Type := QCert.Gen Gate n

/-- Circuits on `n` wires (the Agda's `Circuit n`). -/
abbrev Circ (n : ℕ) : Type := QCert.Circuit Gate n

/-- The cycle as a one-letter circuit, on the bottom three wires. -/
abbrev ω {n : ℕ} : Circ (n + 3) := QCert.Circuit.g3 Gate.ω_gate

/-- Circuits on fewer than three wires have no gate. -/
theorem no_gen₀ : Gn 0 → False
  | .gate0 g => nomatch g

theorem no_gen₁ : Gn 1 → False
  | .gate0 g => nomatch g
  | .gate1 g => nomatch g
  | .up g => no_gen₀ g

theorem no_gen₂ : Gn 2 → False
  | .gate0 g => nomatch g
  | .gate1 g => nomatch g
  | .gate2 g => nomatch g
  | .up g => no_gen₁ g

/-! ## Theories and their congruences -/

/-- Relations on circuits at every width, before the structural rules. -/
abbrev Theory : Type := QCert.CRel Gate

/-! ## Theorem 4's relations

As drawn on p. 267, read in diagram order.  `order` is `ω³ = 1`; `near`
relates two cycles one wire apart, and `far` two cycles two wires apart;
cycles three or more wires apart commute by `Lift.comm3`.  On p. 268 the
same relations are written `ω_i³ = 1` and `ω_i ω_{i+2} ω_i = ω_{i+1} ω_i
ω_{i+2}` (in composition order), with the second one replaced by
`(ω_i ω_{i+1})² = 1`. -/

/-- The Agda's `_SRel,_===_`. -/
inductive SRel : Theory
  | order {n : ℕ} : SRel (n + 3) (ω * ω * ω) 1
  | near {n : ℕ} : SRel (n + 4) (ω * shift ω * ω) (shift ω * shift ω)
  | far {n : ℕ} : SRel (n + 5) (ω * shift (shift ω) * ω) (shift (shift ω) * ω * shift ω)

end QCert.Alt16RevCir.Alternating
